    13	---
    14	
    15	# login
    16	
    17	No path prefix (routes are at the API root, e.g. `/api/v1/login/access-token`).
    18	
    19	| Method | Path | Auth | Notes |
    20	|---|---|---|---|
    21	| POST | `/login/access-token` | none | OAuth2 password flow; returns a `Token` (JWT). See note below. |
    22	| POST | `/login/test-token` | bearer | Returns the current user — a way to verify a token is valid. |
    23	| POST | `/password-recovery/{email}` | none | Always returns the same success `Message` whether or not the email exists (prevents email enumeration). |
    24	| POST | `/reset-password/` | none | Takes the recovery token + new password. |
    25	| POST | `/password-recovery-html-content/{email}` | superuser | Returns the raw recovery email's HTML — for previewing the email template, not part of the actual recovery flow. |
    26	
    27	See [tables/user](../tables/user.md) for the underlying table.
    28	
    29	## Why `/login/access-token` always takes about as long to fail
    30	
    31	`backend/app/crud.py`'s `authenticate()` looks up the user by email; if none is found,
    32	it still runs `verify_password` against a fixed `DUMMY_HASH` before returning `None`.
    33	This is a deliberate timing-attack mitigation, not dead/wasteful code: without it, a
    34	login against a nonexistent email would return faster than one against a real email
    35	with a wrong password, letting response time be used to enumerate valid accounts. If
    36	you're touching `authenticate()`, keep this call even though its result is discarded.
